# Machine-readable citation metadata (https://citation-file-format.github.io/).
# GitHub renders this as a "Cite this repository" button and Zenodo reads it when
# archiving a release.
#
# TWO LICENCES, because there are two things here. The code is MIT (LICENSE,
# committed 2026-10-07). The registry -- registry/*.yaml and docs/*.tsv, which is
# the part with the curation in it -- is CC-BY-4.0 (LICENSE-registry). CFF has
# one `license:` field, so it names the code licence and `license-url` points at
# the pair; a reuser of the DATA needs LICENSE-registry.
#
# This comment block previously said "NO `license:` FIELD YET. The project has
# not chosen one, so there is no LICENSE file either" -- for the 16 days after
# MIT was committed. That made the one machine-readable file GitHub surfaces and
# Zenodo ingests assert all-rights-reserved on a repository that was MIT.
#
# `version:` here is a THIRD copy of the number that lives in assaywatch/__init__.py
# (pyproject.toml reads that one dynamically). Nothing checks this file against it,
# so bump it in the same commit as the tag.
#
# `date-released:` is the tag date. `doi:` is added in the commit after the tag,
# once Zenodo has minted it for the release; the tagged tree therefore predates it. Add `preferred-citation` if a paper appears.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use AssayWatch, please cite it using the metadata below."
title: AssayWatch
abstract: >-
  Continuous in-silico re-validation of published PCR and qPCR diagnostic assays
  against newly deposited GenBank sequences. Assays are designed against the
  handful of sequences available at publication and then used for years while the
  database accumulates divergent strains nobody re-screens them against.
  AssayWatch keeps a version-controlled registry of assays and re-screens it,
  reporting predicted detection by year of deposit and flagging drift. The weekly
  schedule it ships is installed on one machine, so the cadence is a property of
  the tooling rather than of a hosted service.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Nightingale
    given-names: Batya R.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-0706-8951"
repository-code: "https://github.com/batyanight/assaywatch"
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-10-31
license: MIT
# `license:` above names the CODE licence, so this points at the code licence too.
# It pointed at LICENSE-registry until 2026-10-30, which meant this file declared
# MIT and handed a reader the CC-BY text. A reuser of the DATA wants
# LICENSE-registry, named in the abstract and in README.
license-url: "https://github.com/batyanight/assaywatch/blob/main/LICENSE"
keywords:
  - PCR
  - qPCR
  - diagnostic assay
  - assay validation
  - GenBank
  - molecular diagnostics
  - wildlife disease
  - pathogen surveillance
